WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.380
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:09.013
The rain was pouring hard
that day in Brooklyn.

00:00:09.013 --> 00:00:14.642
It was my first day at PS 203,
teaching extra curricular courses.

00:00:14.642 --> 00:00:19.072
200 loud and rambunctious kiddies
run into the cafeteria and

00:00:19.072 --> 00:00:21.047
I tried to settle them down.

00:00:21.047 --> 00:00:25.232
But they were too excited
to begin the new year.

00:00:25.232 --> 00:00:29.961
But one little girl
named Alicia didn't look

00:00:29.961 --> 00:00:34.574
excited at all, in fact, she was in tears.

00:00:34.574 --> 00:00:39.905
I walked over to Alicia and
I asked her what was wrong,

00:00:39.905 --> 00:00:46.082
can't you see it,
she answered sobbing, it's so obvious.

00:00:46.082 --> 00:00:50.130
It wasn't obvious to me,
so I asked her again.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:52.831
My hair, Ms. Oladoyin, it's my hair.

00:00:52.831 --> 00:00:56.939
This morning it was nice and
straight when my mom fixed it, and

00:00:56.939 --> 00:01:01.526
as soon as I got outside in that rain,
poof, my afro came right back.

00:01:01.526 --> 00:01:05.600
And now all the boys
are making fun of me for it.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.885
I told Alicia her hair was beautiful,
but it didn't work.

00:01:10.885 --> 00:01:14.104
He dismissed me with a yeah, whatever.

00:01:14.104 --> 00:01:17.916
It's your job to say that.

00:01:17.916 --> 00:01:21.606
I write multicultural children's books.

00:01:21.606 --> 00:01:27.460
And when I first decided to write these
books, I did it for girls like Alicia.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:30.725
I wanted them to have books I didn't have,
growing up.

00:01:30.725 --> 00:01:34.869
Books that highlight girls
who look like her, and

00:01:34.869 --> 00:01:39.614
have hair like hers,
because representation matters.

00:01:39.614 --> 00:01:44.151
But in the process of creating these
books, I learned something profound.

00:01:44.151 --> 00:01:48.315
It is a beautiful thing when
we can see ourselves in

00:01:48.315 --> 00:01:50.900
the stories of people like us.

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:55.814
But it is a powerful thing
when we can see ourselves in

00:01:55.814 --> 00:01:58.951
the stories of people unlike us.

00:01:58.951 --> 00:02:02.405
Now I know many of us in this room
would probably consider ourselves just

00:02:02.405 --> 00:02:04.665
a little bit older than
my target readership.

00:02:04.665 --> 00:02:07.619
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But I've learned by that by exploring

00:02:07.619 --> 00:02:12.601
diverse narratives such as the ones I
write about, we get that powerful thing.

00:02:12.601 --> 00:02:17.585
The unique ability to unravel
the similarities that can bridge all

00:02:17.585 --> 00:02:19.190
of our differences.

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:23.579
It all started in the exploratory
phase of my creative writing process.

00:02:23.579 --> 00:02:27.600
I did extensive research on all
the cultures I wanted to write about.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.444
I watched very long
educational documentaries.

00:02:30.444 --> 00:02:35.186
I read very dense informative
texts on the tenets of culturally

00:02:35.186 --> 00:02:38.597
relevant pedagogy, but it wasn't enough.

00:02:38.597 --> 00:02:41.239
Something was missing.

00:02:41.239 --> 00:02:43.535
Writing for
children is a funny experience, you see.

00:02:43.535 --> 00:02:47.039
I went to PS 203 so
I could learn how they learn.

00:02:47.039 --> 00:02:50.580
I wanted to observe how they would
engage with this kind of content.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.380
I wanted to teach them about people and
places so

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:56.544
that they could be curious
about our diverse planet.

00:02:56.544 --> 00:02:59.056
What I discovered was this.

00:02:59.056 --> 00:03:01.776
Although I knew it was very important for

00:03:01.776 --> 00:03:06.666
them to grow up understanding these
concepts, they didn't know that.

00:03:06.666 --> 00:03:08.575
And they didn't have to know that.

00:03:08.575 --> 00:03:12.723
Because when you're five,
six, seven years old,

00:03:12.723 --> 00:03:17.072
all you want when you pick up
a book is a real good story.

00:03:17.072 --> 00:03:18.734
And that was it.

00:03:18.734 --> 00:03:24.483
At the core of my multicultural
educational exposure mission,

00:03:24.483 --> 00:03:28.322
I needed to harness
the power of the story.

00:03:28.322 --> 00:03:30.538
So I picked up my research process again.

00:03:30.538 --> 00:03:35.122
This time, I interviewed as many people
as I could find from those cultures and

00:03:35.122 --> 00:03:36.238
those countries.

00:03:36.238 --> 00:03:39.193
And I just listened to their stories.

00:03:39.193 --> 00:03:42.564
I took note of every
little detail they shared,

00:03:42.564 --> 00:03:45.862
especially the ones their
faces lit up about.

00:03:45.862 --> 00:03:49.989
Like when I was creating Chizelle, my
girl character from Trinidad and Tobago.

00:03:49.989 --> 00:03:54.252
I interviewed a family of Trinidadian
immigrants and they told me about the tree

00:03:54.252 --> 00:03:58.401
that they had in their backyard when
they were growing up in the countryside.

00:03:58.401 --> 00:04:01.180
Which is different from the kind
of tree that you would see if you

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:03.548
only visit the city with all
the rest of the tourists.

00:04:03.548 --> 00:04:07.867
And they chuckled about how they would
play with their siblings underneath that

00:04:07.867 --> 00:04:10.524
tree and
shake it til all the coconuts fell down.

00:04:10.524 --> 00:04:15.839
A seemingly insignificant detail at
first glance, maybe, but it is in those

00:04:15.839 --> 00:04:21.341
details that I instantly felt connected
to someone I thought was so different.

00:04:21.341 --> 00:04:25.865
I may not have grown up in Trinidad, but
growing up in a suburban part of Brooklyn,

00:04:25.865 --> 00:04:27.636
also with an immigrant family,

00:04:27.636 --> 00:04:31.192
I knew all about playing with my
siblings underneath big trees.

00:04:31.192 --> 00:04:34.136
And it is upon those little details and

00:04:34.136 --> 00:04:38.559
those little connections,
that I built an entire book.

00:04:38.559 --> 00:04:44.212
And for every book I wrote, I listened
to dozens more of these stories.

00:04:44.212 --> 00:04:49.911
And from India to Colombia, I found
myself in each of their narratives.

00:04:49.911 --> 00:04:52.923
This enabled my writing
to go beyond any fact, or

00:04:52.923 --> 00:04:56.302
any figure, that I could find online or
in a textbook or

00:04:56.302 --> 00:05:00.278
even in my own personal travel
experiences to those countries.

00:05:00.278 --> 00:05:05.429
Most of all it gave me that skill that
can be applied to anyone in this room.

00:05:05.429 --> 00:05:10.930
I learned how to relate with
people who do not look like me,

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:16.116
to hear them, to understand them,
to work with them.

00:05:16.116 --> 00:05:18.142
Maybe you're not planning on writing or

00:05:18.142 --> 00:05:21.189
reading multi-cultural
children's books any time soon.

00:05:21.189 --> 00:05:26.335
How can you harness the power of the story
to build your own empathy bridge?

00:05:26.335 --> 00:05:29.004
You can start by reading a book,
any book, fact or

00:05:29.004 --> 00:05:31.941
fiction about someone from
another part of the world.

00:05:31.941 --> 00:05:35.601
Enter their journey as
though it were yours.

00:05:35.601 --> 00:05:41.008
See just how different or
similar the world is from their eyes.

00:05:41.008 --> 00:05:45.596
What I love about the power of the story
is that it's not just on the pages of

00:05:45.596 --> 00:05:47.381
a book, it's everywhere.

00:05:47.381 --> 00:05:50.236
We just have to recognize and
appreciate when we see it.

00:05:50.236 --> 00:05:54.816
So maybe that means you'll finally
sit down with that classmate or

00:05:54.816 --> 00:05:57.697
that coworker that you pass by everyday.

00:05:57.697 --> 00:06:00.537
You exchange a routine hi,
how are you doing?

00:06:00.537 --> 00:06:01.300
I'm fine.

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:03.878
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You see them, sure,

00:06:03.878 --> 00:06:05.433
but do you know their story?

00:06:05.433 --> 00:06:10.552
Or it could be something as simple
as watching a film from a foreign

00:06:10.552 --> 00:06:15.498
country in another language and
see how it resonates with you.

00:06:15.498 --> 00:06:19.322
My personal favorite, Korean dramas.

00:06:19.322 --> 00:06:21.896
I love Korean dramas.
I'm not ashamed.

00:06:21.896 --> 00:06:26.042
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Perhaps none of these concepts

00:06:26.042 --> 00:06:28.093
are new to you.

00:06:28.093 --> 00:06:32.775
It's no surprise in the polarized
world we live in today that there is

00:06:32.775 --> 00:06:37.471
indeed an empathy gap across our
communities that we need to bridge.

00:06:37.471 --> 00:06:40.249
Why aren't we bridging it?

00:06:40.249 --> 00:06:45.896
Fear, we are afraid to move
away from what is familiar.

00:06:45.896 --> 00:06:49.680
Think about the people you surround
yourself with all the time.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:55.474
Do you constantly find yourself in
the comfort zone of a homogeneous creed,

00:06:55.474 --> 00:06:58.199
class, color, fill in the blank.

00:06:58.199 --> 00:07:00.461
These safe spaces are important,

00:07:00.461 --> 00:07:04.604
but the problem is it's often
where the least growth happens.

00:07:04.604 --> 00:07:08.889
But if we can embrace the vulnerability
that comes with sharing our stories, and

00:07:08.889 --> 00:07:12.984
the courage needed to seek out the stories
of others, we can build empathy for

00:07:12.984 --> 00:07:13.630
our peers.

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:16.653
We can shatter misconceptions, one by one.

00:07:16.653 --> 00:07:21.445
We can better understand each other,
better understand how to serve each other.

00:07:21.445 --> 00:07:25.875
We can heighten our awareness of
those that are right around us that

00:07:25.875 --> 00:07:27.784
we ignore every single day.

00:07:29.939 --> 00:07:33.636
Halfway through that
extra-curricular course at PS 203,

00:07:33.636 --> 00:07:38.518
I laid out the final character development
of my premier collection of children's

00:07:38.518 --> 00:07:41.532
books before my esteemed
panel of fourth graders.

00:07:41.532 --> 00:07:44.719
One of them being Alicia.

00:07:44.719 --> 00:07:48.672
I like her.

00:07:48.672 --> 00:07:52.772
Alicia pointed to Akua my
Ghanaian girl character.

00:07:52.772 --> 00:07:54.443
And she pointed emphatically.

00:07:54.443 --> 00:07:55.854
So I was intrigued.

00:07:55.854 --> 00:08:00.099
I asked Alisha what she
liked most about Akua.

00:08:00.099 --> 00:08:02.702
I like her hair, Alicia responded.

00:08:02.702 --> 00:08:03.838
It's foxy.

00:08:03.838 --> 00:08:07.816
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I laughed, trying to remind Alicia of

00:08:07.816 --> 00:08:11.016
the crying incident that happened
just a few weeks earlier.

00:08:11.016 --> 00:08:14.599
But Alicia said she had no
recollection whatsoever.

00:08:14.599 --> 00:08:18.691
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; She had just finished all about

00:08:18.691 --> 00:08:21.838
Akua's journey from
Cape Coast to Accra and

00:08:21.838 --> 00:08:27.379
felt especially connected to the book's
references on art and friendship.

00:08:27.379 --> 00:08:33.478
But when I asked Alicia why she loved Akua
so much, she said something very simple.

00:08:33.478 --> 00:08:38.073
It's because I am she and she is me.

00:08:38.073 --> 00:08:42.555
I pressed, arguing that she had never
even heard of Ghana prior to that day.

00:08:42.555 --> 00:08:45.090
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And many of the cultural references in

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:47.036
that book were brand new to her.

00:08:47.036 --> 00:08:51.660
But she insisted,
it doesn't matter Ms. Oladoyin.

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:55.392
I am she and she is me.

00:08:55.392 --> 00:08:59.393
So whenever I get tempted to
revert to what is comfortable,

00:08:59.393 --> 00:09:03.404
to those who are familiar,
I remember the words of Alicia.

00:09:03.404 --> 00:09:10.099
I restate them to myself back to
the world, that I am they and they are me.

00:09:10.099 --> 00:09:10.908
Thank you.

00:09:10.908 --> 00:09:16.498
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:16.498 --> 00:09:19.948
[MUSIC]

